      "claim": "C10 shows a brain/plasma ratio of 0.69 in rat, but the compound is a lipophilic pyridinone-pyrrolidine with a single basic amine; this permeable phenotype is not transferable to the dicationic guanidine-azocane query, which is fully ionized with logD -2.7.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "C06 shows a small basic piperazine crosses the BBB in rats, but it is not a dicationic guanidine; the query's complete ionization and very low lipophilicity present a much larger barrier, so this result does not override the fail prediction.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "C03 (piracetam) is polar yet reaches a brain/serum ratio of 2, but it is a small neutral amide rather than a dicationic guanidine; its brain entry does not provide transferable evidence for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "C02 reports high brain/plasma ratios for biperiden, but this is a highly lipophilic anticholinergic with a single basic amine and multiple hydrophobic rings; the query lacks the lipophilic surface needed to offset its dicationic charge.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is guanethidine, a dicationic guanidine-azocane that is essentially fully ionized at physiological pH and has very low lipophilicity. The supplied permeable analogues are all lipophilic basic amines lacking the guanidine dication; their CNS penetration does not transfer. Known pharmacology of guanethidine indicates negligible BBB penetration and peripheral sympatholytic action, consistent with the low passive-diffusion prior and high efflux liability. Therefore, meaningful CNS access is not expected.",
